aVAccession Number:  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status: Final \u000aType: Surgical Pathology \u000aPathology Report  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  SKIN- OTHER THAN CYST, TAG OR DEBRIDEMENT \u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aDORFMAN, DAVID, MICHAEL, M.D.~PH.D. \u000aDIAGNOSIS: by DORFMAN,DAVID MICHAEL,M.D.,PH.D. \u000aSKIN, LEFT BREAST: \u000aSkin showing a mild superficial interstitial lymphoid infiltrate \u000awith minimal cytologic atypia. There are admixed histiocytes present. \u000aVery focal epidermotropism is identified. The dermis shows sclerosis \u000aand mild vascular ectasia. \u000aImmunoperoxidase studies performed on formalin fixed paraffin sections \u000areveal the majority of cells are reactive for T cell markers  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ,  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID , \u000aand  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID . Only occasional cells show  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  reactivity. There is a mixture \u000aof  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  positive cells. Approximately 50% of the cells are \u000areactive for  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID .  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  stain highlights only a few cells.  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  stain \u000ais negative. \u000aNOTE: \u000aThe findings raise the possibility of a T cell lymphoproliferative \u000adisorder but are not diagnostic. Tissue will be submitted for gene \u000arearrangement studies and the results will be reported in an addendum. \u000aThis case has been reviewed by Dr. Philip McKee, Dermatopathologist. \u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: by LINDEMAN,NEAL I.,M.D. \u000aDNA was isolated from frozen skin (left breast) and analyzed by polymerase \u000achain reaction, using consensus primers to the V and J regions of the T Cell \u000areceptor gamma chain gene. \u000aResults: A single, intense, discrete band was obtained with primers to Vgamma \u000aregion 11, identical in REMOVED_DATE aliquots. A reproducible pattern of multiple faint \u000abands was obtained with primers to region Vg9, and smears were obtained with \u000aprimers to regions Vg1-8, Vg10. \u000aThese results are consistent with a monoclonal T-cell population. \u000aCLINICAL DATA: \u000aHistory: 58 year old white female with 3 month history of asymptomatic atrophic \u000atelangiectatic plaques on medial breasts and proximal thighs. \u000aOperation: Excision \u000aOperative findings: Repeat biopsy for tissue markers \u000aClinical Diagnosis: Rule out CTCL vs lymphoproliferative disorder. \u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: Skin - left breast. \u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by O'CONNELL,FIONNUALA \u000aThe specimen is received in saline,  labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient's name and unit \u000anumber only, and consists of a skin punch measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0.3cm which is \u000abisected. One piece is fixed in formalin and one piece is snap frozen for \u000apotential gene rearrangement studies and/or immunoperoxidase studies. \u000aMicro 1: 1 frag, ESS. \u000aA second specimen is received in formalin on REMOVED_DATE   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  \u000apatient's name and unit number only, and consists of a skin punch measuring \u000a0.3 x REMOVED_DATE x 0.3cm  \u000aMicro 2: 1 frag., ESS. \u000aReports to: O'SULLIVAN-HUNNEWELL, REBECCA \u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: SKIN- OTHER THAN CYST, TAG OR DEBRIDEMENT \u000aADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE: SKIN- OTHER THAN CYST, TAG OR DEBRIDEMENT \u000a[report_end] 
