he biopsy site. There is a firm nodule adjacent to the biopsy \u000asite that measures 1.2cm in greatest dimension with central hemorrhage. The \u000anodule is not distinguished from the surrounding fibrous tissue. \u000aA clip was detected by the radiograph, submitted with the specimen. Following \u000asectioning, the specimen is re-radiographed by the pathology resident to reveal \u000athe clip. Due to the fibrotic and hemorrhagic nature of the excision specimen \u000awith such extensive fibrous areas involving margins, the specimen is entirely \u000asubmitted. \u000aMicros  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Breast excision, REMOVED_DATE frags in each, RSS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Breast slice with area of clip, 1 frag each, ESS. \u000aMicros  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Breast with overlying skin ellipse, 1 frag each, ESS. \u000aMicros  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Remainder of breast excision specimen, REMOVED_DATE frags each, ESS. \u000aPES/mmm \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aResident Review by Peter M Sadow M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by William R Welch M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end] 
