ograph which demonstrates a wire and an area of calcifications in the \u000aspecimen. The specimen has been previously inked per the surgeon per pathology \u000aprotocol (superior/red, inferior/green, medial/orange, lateral/yellow, \u000aanterior/blue, and posterior/black), and there is a needle localization wire in \u000aplace, which enters through the lateral aspect of the specimen and exits \u000athrough the medial aspect of the specimen. The specimen is serially sectioned \u000afrom medial to lateral and the cut surface is tan/yellow and homogeneous with \u000afocal areas of dense white fibrous tissue constituting approximately less than \u000a2% of the breast parenchyma. No discrete lesions or areas of disruption are \u000agrossly identified. The specimen is submitted in its entirety. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Medial margin, 3 frags, ESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Sequential cross sections, medial to lateral, blocks  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  to \u000ainclude possible area of calcification per specimen radiograph, 1 frag each, \u000aESS. \u000aMicro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID : Lateral margin, 3 frags, ESS. \u000aCASE NUMBER:  REMOVED_CASE_ID . \u000asek/lp \u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she \u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated) \u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es) \u000arelated thereto. \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on \u000a9/3/ REMOVED_CASE_ID  \u000a[report_end] 
